The intention of adding the "Macroable" trait to Pennant was to enable something like the following....

```php
Feature::macro('forSession', function () {
    return $this->for('session|'.Session::getId());
});


Feature::forSession()->active();
```

See: https://github.com/laravel/pennant/pull/18

To achieve this, the Macro needs to be on the decorator otherwise the `'session|id-here'` would be appended to the scope array, which will already contain the default scope if it isn't `null`.

There may be a use-case for making the pending interaction macroable, but I've removed it until we have a real-world example.

